      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2017 The American College of Veterinary Surgeons.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To investigate the strength and size of surgeon's and square knots for starting and ending continuous suture lines using large gauge suture."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "In vitro mechanical study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY POPULATION",
            "content": "Knotted suture."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Surgeon's and square knots were tested using 2 and 3 USP polyglactin 910 and 2 USP polydioxanone under linear tension on a universal testing machine. Failure mode and knot holding capacity (KHC) were recorded, and relative knot security (RKS) was calculated as a percentage of KHC. Comparisons were made between number of throws, suture size, suture type, and knot types. Knot volume and weight were assessed by a digital micrometer and balance, respectively."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "There were no significant differences in KHC (P\u2009=\u2009.295), RKS (P\u2009=\u2009.307), volume (P\u2009=\u2009.128), or weight (P\u2009=\u2009.310) between square and surgeon's knots at the start or end of suture lines with the same number of throws and suture type. A minimum of 6 throws were required for start knots and 7 throws at end knots to prevent unraveling. Knots tied with 3 polyglactin 910 were strongest (P\u2009<\u2009.001) and 2 polyglactin 910 produced knots with higher KHC and RKS than 2 polydioxanone (P\u2009<\u2009.001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "No consistent differences were detected between knots types tied with the same suture material; however, number of throws affected KHC and RKS up to 6 throws in start or 7 throws in end knots. The configuration of square and surgeon's knots performed at the end of a continuous line alters their KHC, supporting the use of additional throws for knot security."
          }
        ]
      },
